# UI test: `[1 2]`, an array literal whose elements are not separated
# (SC0100).
#
# This is a no-cascade case rather than a new code. The list reports the
# missing separator itself, where both tokens are in scope, and then recovers
# to the `]`; if it left the complaint to the `expect` that closes the
# bracket, the statement would fail a second time when the line did not end,
# and one mistake would print twice. That is exactly the failure this whole
# change was written to remove — `let xs be [1, 2]` used to print `SC0105`
# and then `SC0100` — so reintroducing it one bracket over would be a poor
# trade.
#
# The binding after it parses clean, which is the other half of the claim.

def main():
    let xs be [1 2]
    let ys be [3, 4]
    print(ys)
